  "about": "Who may do what in a space, as DATA evaluated by the shared src/rule-decisions.mjs owner. Every check in a space states one `action` and the facts about the person asking and the thing they act on; each rule is { id, effect, says (what it means, as the space shows it), refuses (a deny's words to the person it stops; the action's own `refused` words when left out), who, actions, when, until? }, where `who` is one of `who_kinds` (a member type is the id in the member's `role` in that space, `member` when it has none; in a group or project the spaces screens own the ids as DATA, in a community they are its configured roles, whose `may` lists read as the fact `type_may`), `actions` lists the action ids it covers (every action when left out) and `when` is an optional rule tree over those facts in the formula engine's shape (parts/formula-engine/rules/compile-rules.mjs; comparisons from rule-operators.json). Effects and their precedence are declared below; a deny outranks an allow, and without a winning allow the action is refused with its own `refused` words. A rule holds when its who, actions and when all hold; `until` expires it. A rule that is not finished refuses everything in its space. `default_rules` is what every space and community follows until someone replaces its rules (a space with no rules of its own reads these, so a change here reaches every such space); `retired_default_identities` are the BLAKE3 identities of earlier starting rules, and a space still holding one of those exactly goes back to following `default_rules`; the rules may be replaced by whoever the space's own rules let do `change_rules`. `facts` are what a rule may name; `__policy_rule_id` is reserved by the evaluator. A new action is one row in `actions` and a check that names it; a new fact is one row in `facts` and the value the check hands in. A fact's `values` (or `values_from`: `actions`, `member_types` or `members`, read from the space) are what a rule may compare it with, as the rule builder offers them. An action's `offered_on` says where a screen asks whether the person may do it: `space` (once per space read, as `may`) or `item` (once per thing, as the thing's `may`). Mandatory invariant_rules are evaluated before selected Space rules. They rely on actual admitted source authority; a Space cannot retain another account’s private source by removing its membership or changing its rules.",
